In Kubernetes, troubleshooting means finding why an application/pod is not working and fixing the problem.

A good interview approach is:
Pod → Events → Logs → Service → Ingress → Node → Resources → Storage → Configuration


1. Check the Pods first   kubectl get pods
For a specific namespace:  kubectl get pods -n dev
For more details:  kubectl get pods -o wide

2. Describe the Pod   kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

This is one of the most important troubleshooting commands.

Look mainly at:
Events:
For example:
FailedScheduling
ImagePullBackOff
ErrImagePull
FailedMount
CrashLoopBackOff

Events often tell you why Kubernetes is having a problem.


Events means Kubernetes keeps a record of important things that happened to a resource, especially things related to errors or scheduling.
For example, run:  kubectl describe pod nginx

At the bottom you may see:
Events:
  Type     Reason            Message
  ----     ------            -------
  Normal   Scheduled         Successfully assigned pod to node
  Normal   Pulling           Pulling image "nginx"
  Normal   Pulled            Successfully pulled image
  Warning  FailedMount       Unable to mount volume

Simple meaning   Think of Events as Kubernetes' activity/error history for that Pod.

Scheduled → Pod was assigned to a node
Pulling → Kubernetes is downloading the image
Pulled → Image downloaded successfully
Failed → Something failed
FailedMount → Storage could not be mounted
FailedScheduling → Kubernetes couldn't find a suitable node
BackOff → Kubernetes is repeatedly trying again

So during troubleshooting:
Pod not working
      ↓
kubectl describe pod
      ↓
Check Events
      ↓
Find the reason for the problem

Interview answer:  "Events show the important activities, warnings, and errors that Kubernetes records for a resource. 
I check them using kubectl describe to understand why a Pod or other resource is not working."


Kubernetes Events vs Logs

The easiest way to remember:
Events = What Kubernetes is doing
Logs = What the application/container is doing

	               Events	                                 Logs
Comes from    	Kubernetes	                      Application/container
Tells you    	Kubernetes-level activities/errors	Application output/errors
Example         	FailedScheduling               	Database connection refused
Command	          kubectl describe pod <pod>          	kubectl logs <pod>
Useful for    	Pod scheduling, image pulling, volumes, probes	       Application crashes, exceptions, errors

Example
Suppose your Pod is:  CrashLoopBackOff
First:  kubectl describe pod myapp
Events might show:   Warning  BackOff  Back-off restarting failed container

This tells you Kubernetes is repeatedly restarting the container.

Then:  kubectl logs myapp
You might see:  ERROR: Unable to connect to database
This tells you why the application is crashing.

If you specifically want all Events
kubectl get events

So:
kubectl describe pod → Pod details + Events
kubectl describe svc → Service details + Events
kubectl get events → Events directly


kubectl get events shows the events/activities that Kubernetes has recorded in the current namespace.
Example:  kubectl get events

You might see:
LAST SEEN   TYPE      REASON             OBJECT              MESSAGE
2m          Normal    Scheduled          pod/nginx            Successfully assigned pod to node1
2m          Normal    Pulling            pod/nginx            Pulling image "nginx"
1m          Normal    Pulled             pod/nginx            Successfully pulled image
1m          Normal    Started            pod/nginx            Started container nginx
30s         Warning   FailedScheduling   pod/myapp            0/2 nodes are available
What each column means
LAST SEEN → When the event happened
TYPE → Normal or Warning
REASON → What happened, e.g. FailedScheduling
OBJECT → Which resource had the event, e.g. Pod
MESSAGE → Detailed explanation

For troubleshooting, the REASON + MESSAGE are especially important.
You can also see events from a specific namespace:  kubectl get events -n dev
So simply:  kubectl get events = Show Kubernetes events so we can understand what is happening or what went wrong.




3. Check Pod logs  kubectl logs <pod-name>
If the Pod has multiple containers:  kubectl logs <pod-name> -c <container-name>
For previous crashed container logs:    kubectl logs <pod-name> --previous


7. Pod is Running but application is not accessible
Now check the Service.
kubectl get svc

Then: kubectl describe svc <service-name>   also shows Events at the bottom.


Check Endpoints
kubectl get endpoints <service-name>

If you get:  ENDPOINTS: <none>
the Service is not finding the Pods.
Usually the problem is a selector/label mismatch.

Example Service:
selector:
  app: nginx

Pod:
labels:
  app: nginx

These must match.


For a Service problem, you would typically check:  kubectl describe svc nginx-service
and especially:
Selector
Endpoints
Ports


8. Test the Service from inside the cluster

You can enter a Pod:  kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- sh
Then:  curl http://nginx-service:80

This helps determine whether the problem is:
Application → Service
or
Service → outside/Ingress


9. Ingress problem

Check:  kubectl get ingress
Then:  kubectl describe ingress <ingress-name>

Check:
Host
Path
Backend Service
Service port
Ingress controller
DNS
TLS certificate

Also check the Ingress Controller:  kubectl get pods -n ingress-nginx
and:  kubectl logs -n ingress-nginx <controller-pod>


10. Check Nodes
kubectl get nodes

You want:
STATUS
Ready

If:  NotReady
investigate:  kubectl describe node <node-name>
Also check:   kubectl get events


11. Check CPU and Memory

kubectl top pods                       shows how much CPU and memory each Pod is currently using.
For nodes:  kubectl top nodes          shows CPU and memory usage of each node.
If a Pod is consuming too much memory:

NAME       CPU     MEMORY
app-pod    500m    900Mi

you may need to check its resource configuration:

resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "100m"
    memory: "128Mi"

  limits:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "512Mi"

If memory exceeds the limit, the container may be OOMKilled.




If you mean to continuously see live CPU and memory usage, use:  watch kubectl top pods
It will refresh the output automatically.
For nodes:  watch kubectl top nodes




















